  "title": "relational modeling constraints data types null and normalization",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "A relational model represents facts as relations with declared keys and constraints. Table design begins by identifying entities, candidate identifiers, attributes, and relationships; constraints make invalid states harder to store.",
    "Normalization separates facts according to functional dependencies to reduce update, insertion, and deletion anomalies. Denormalization is a deliberate read or performance choice that introduces consistency work and should preserve an authoritative source.",
    "Data types encode domain meaning, range, precision, collation, and storage behavior. Choosing a type that merely accepts a value can silently change comparisons, rounding, sorting, or null semantics.",
    "NULL represents missing or unknown information and changes predicate logic: comparisons can evaluate to UNKNOWN. It should not be used as a substitute for zero, empty text, or a missing relationship without a clear domain meaning.",
    "Constraints such as PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK, and NOT NULL define enforceable invariants. Application validation improves feedback but does not replace constraints at the database boundary."
  ],
